  % Some mix Cajun-marked and Creole-marked features even within a single paradigm, such as Gene's use of the Cajun-marked \lexi{je} for 1sg but also the Creole-marked \lexi{yé} for 3pl.
  % These speakers, while they have been acknowledged to exist by researchers who focus on Louisiana, are nonetheless left out of descriptions of Creole because their speech is not seen as Creole enough.
  % On the other hand, they are also left out of descriptions of French as their speech is likewise not seen as French enough.
  % This study, however, not only acknowledges the existence of these speakers, as well as the fuzzy boundaries between what have been called French and Creole, but has offered a robust structural analysis of the whole range of F\&C subject pronouns without excluding those whose speech does not fit neatly into the boundaries of French or Creole that have been set out.
  % The only other study to approach this sort of inclusion has been \textcite{klingler_language_2003}.
  % More work can of course be done in this direction as well as in other directions towards which this study hints, though.

  Looking at broader implications, this has been a raciolinguistics study in the sense that it has focused on ethnicity and race with a raciolinguistic perspective in mind.
  The importance of not siloing off raciolinguistics from sociolinguistics as a separate track is indeed something against which \textcite{flores_undoing_2023} have cautioned, so while this study adds to the growing body of raciolinguistic-informed work, it more generally adds to how race and ethnicity are understood in sociolinguistics.
  In short, ethnic and racialized categories are not fixed nor experienced the same by everyone.
  Where both are implicated, they intersect with each other as well as with other aspects of identity.
  Creole and Cajun identities were thus seen here to be highly associated with Blackness and Whiteness, respectively, but those associations were not categorical.
  Place and sociohistorical developments also appeared to play a role in how these identities were constructed and understood.
  
  Subject pronouns were used to express that intersectionality much as other linguistic features have been found to do in other sociolinguistic contexts such as the \vowel{bat} vowel \parencite{king_rethinking_2021} for African Americans with different orientations to place and /s/ fronting \parencite{calder_whose_2022} for Black and White men and women.
  The former study is particularly relevant here.
  It showed that those young African Americans professionals who had a desire to leave Rochester, NY for better work opportunities produced lowered and retracted \vowel{bat} vowels, a reversal of the Northern Vowels Shift, even compared to other African Americans professionals of the same generation who instead felt secure in their professions in Rochester.
  In effect, African Americans could be understood in relation to each other rather than in relation to White speakers only in that they had different orientations to place that led them to adopt or reject local vowel patterns.
  Likewise, Cajuns in the present study could be understood in relation to each other with recourse to the effects of place just as Creoles especially could be.
  Gene could produce meta-commentary on the differences between Creoles' F\&C inside and outside of the Scott-Carencro-Lafayette area, and Owen could express a stance towards other Creoles from far northwest of him by juxtaposing them with subject pronouns that do not index his own locality.
  While Creoles and Cajuns are socially understood relative to each other, as well, members of neither group are defined wholly by their ethnicity.

