  \section{Limitations and future directions}
    The future directions that the current research can take come mostly from its limitations.
    A number of these are relevant to the quantitative analysis of subject pronouns, but others are relevant to the social network analysis and the discourse analysis, as well.
    None of these invalidate the work, but they do harken to the need for study replications as well as expanding the research to flesh out what has already been found here.

    One overarching limitation is that the sample of participants was not a true random sample that could produce generalizable results.
    This is the primary case to be made for the need for replication studies.
    More specifically related to the results for subject pronouns, though, is that the broad US categories of Black and White may be even more predictive of subject pronoun usage than the local categories of Creole and Cajun.
    While this finding has been useful in and of itself, more can be done to understand the role that broad US racial categories have in predicting how subject pronouns vary between Creoles and Cajuns.
    There was a clear association of Cajuns with White and Creoles with Black, but the US categories provided by participants were simplified due to the size of the sample of participants.
    Alternatively, as \textcite{giancarlo_dont_2019} did for Creoles of color, it would likely be fruitful to examine through a variationist lens a large sample of Creoles and even of Cajuns that can capture considerable counts for racialized identities other than Black and White.
    Indeed, while Cajuns were more straightforwardly White in their self-identifications, they too varied in their racialized identities, primarily by sometimes including \lexi{Cajun} itself as part of if not the whole of their racial self-identifications.
    Likewise, not all Creoles were ``of color'', in broad US terms, despite the category's strong association with Blackness.

    Creoles and Cajuns may also differ in their linguistic behavior relative to place.
    The results here consistently hinted at the importance of place, much like \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}'s (\citeyear{rottet_language_1995}) results in Terrebonne-Lafourche did for Indians and Cajuns whose ethnic identities were likely correlated with place.
    The effect of place could not be quantitatively detected here, though, because the sample of participants was not large enough to have a sufficient count of both Creoles and Cajuns in each of several towns nor even in each of several parishes.
    Such an analysis would likely have to use the level of towns as a delimiter rather than parishes as, anecdotally, there appeared to be differences even between neighboring towns.
    For example, Arnaudville seemed more likely to have speakers with Cajun-marked pronouns, whereas Cecilia, only seven miles southeast, was repeatedly described as having speakers of Creole.

    Gathering such a number of participants from several towns each would be a rather large task, however, likely requiring extensive funding.
    A more practical route for conducting a partial replication of the current study would be to limit the sample to many participants in a single town.
    The towns of St Martinville and Breaux Bridge would be ideal choices.
    The former is home to two Cajun-centric heritage sites, the Acadian Memorial and the Evangeline Oak, which are juxtaposed against an African American museum that gives a central place to Creoles in its exhibits.
    St Martinville also plays up the presence of F\&C with significant French -- but not Creole -- signage throughout the central area of the town \parencite{mcneill_bilingualism_2024}.
    Breaux Bridge, on the other hand, was the site of one of the most important documentations of Creole in the state \parencite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}, and several current participants were associated with this town.
    One of those participants was Errol, a White Cajun who used Creole-marked pronouns.
    Interactions with locals who did not become participants suggested that Errol's linguistic behavior relative to his identity was not unusual in Breaux Bridge.
    Other potentially fruitful towns to examine would be Henderson and Cecilia, although these would perhaps be more difficult to study.
    For Cecilia, the difficulty would be that there is a lack of obvious public gathering locations where participants may be found.
    Henderson has popular public gathering locations in the restaurant Pat's Fisherman Wharf and the adjoining dance hall, the Atchafalaya Club.
    However, my own visits to the town and interactions with the people suggested that the people there are particularly wary of strangers asking about their language, and Henderson also has a relatively low Black population relative to the other towns mentioned according to census records.

    Any further variationist analyses of F\&C should also aim to include a full range of ages despite the growing difficulty in doing so.
    \textcite{rottet_language_1995} was able to use a middle age range (\foreign{i.e.}, 30-54) as a bin, and while \textcite{dajko_ethnic_2009} chose to focus on speakers over 60 due in part to difficulties in finding younger Cajun speakers, she did report finding Indians in their 30s who only learned English once they began school (p.~65).
    However, the present study had a noticeable gap in the middle age ranges despite making no attempts to prioritize older individuals.
    There are two possible reasons for this.
    There may be a lack of speakers in that age range due to how the valuation of F\&C has shifted back and forth over time.
    A very common personal story for those under 40 or even under 50 is recalling how their grandparents spoke F\&C but not their parents, a story that is even applicable to myself.
    The younger people that I did find who spoke F\&C were either Black, products of immersion schooling, or particularly motivated individuals who had taken it upon themselves to teach themselves the language.
    For these last two categories, \textcite{gudmestad_variationist_2022}, in agreement with \textcite{kasstan_emergent_2019}, suggested that researchers should look at the speech of speakers who have learned French through revitalization programs, so-called new speakers (p.~21), a view that is also shared here.
    The other possible explanation is that those in the middle age ranges are likely to be busier than older folks.
    This means they may have less time available, be less willing to share what time they do have available, and/or are not spending as much time in public locations or at public events where they might be recruited for participation.

    On the social network front, there is a dearth of such data for Louisiana, especially social network data that codes for local social categories.
    Part of the aim of this study was to begin the process of filling in this gap in information.
    The social network data presented here gives a consistent image of Creoles primarily associating with other Creoles and almost never with Cajuns and of Cajuns primarily associating with other Cajuns and only rarely taking on Creoles as acquaintances.
    This is valuable information but not yet generalizable, suggesting the need for social network data collection to continue.
    
    Social network data collection is difficult to get right, however, and in the case of collecting whole network data, it is very time consuming.
    There are indications here, though, that speakers of F\&C, especially Cajuns, have relatively short social distances between them.
    The continued collection of this data can provide a much better picture of those distances as the whole network comes to be elaborated.
    Having such data would allow for studies focusing in on social structure, as well.
    For instance, some network analyses apply only to whole networks, such as centrality measures and community detection techniques that might shed light on speakers' linguistic behaviors when variables such as ethnicity, place, and social class are unable to render social positions and organization transparent.

    Beyond research, though, social network data may be informative for how to approach F\&C revitalization efforts.
    It is common for F\&C speakers to report having no one with whom to speak the language, but even the very fact that participants can be successfully recruited for studies such as this one is evidence that there are often F\&C speakers hiding in plain sight.
    Knowing more about this can provide avenues to target when attempting to put F\&C speakers into greater contact with each other.
    Network data is also effective for uncovering where languages are being passed along, whether to children, grandchildren, or to any other connection.
    Such knowledge is essential for building effective revitalization programs, especially when resources for such programs are as limited as they are in Louisiana and so cannot be wasted on ineffective initiatives.
  
    Finally, the discourse data presented here was particularly rich but also all collected by myself, a White man whose connections to Louisiana are tangental rather than direct.
    Adjustments to the interviewer variable by employing interviewers with different backgrounds may yield more variation in how speakers use subject pronouns in F\&C, as has long been found in studies of speakers in other communities \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][]{rickford_addressee-_1994}.
    Likewise, group interviews could facilitate observations of such variation while also giving participants more opportunities to produce different 2sg pronouns as these could not be easily studied with the methods used in the present study.
    
    \textcite{wendte_tale_2020} did not actively attempt to conduct such group interviews, but just as it happened from time to time in the present study, he did conduct some.
    One of those group interviews provided a striking example of just how valuable this sort of data can be.
    Two of the three participants in the exchange were reported as identifying as White Cajuns except when they were in the presence of the third participant, a Black friend, at which point they identified as Creoles.
    This Black friend, however, while in their company, defined Cajuns as Frenchmen instead of White and then claimed to not know her own identity when asked.
    Instead, she looked to the other two for permission to identify as Creole and denied that she could be Cajun, as well, like her friends could (pp.~293-294).
    Unfortunately, the Black Creole participant in this exchange only responded in English while her friends used Cajun-marked subject pronouns, but the potential for observing how subject pronoun choices play out in such discourse would provide important information that cannot be obtained in one-on-one interviews.
    
    The current study has added a great deal to the understanding of Creoles, Cajuns and their subject pronoun systems in F\&C.
    It has also added more generally to how race and ethnicity are understood in sociolinguistics.
    Replications, expansions, and adjustments to methods would all, however, be beneficial in filling out the picture uncovered here.